R.append((r"Part (iv) holds because the gain is a common non-negative scalar times $\tau(y)$.",
          r"Part (iv): with quasilinear utility and the price of $S$ fixed, a change in factor income is the household's equivalent variation. The gain relative to income is $\Delta w\,\tau(y)/y$ with $\Delta w=w(p_c^1)-w(p_c^0)>0$ by (i), so it is non-increasing in $y$ if and only if $\tau(y)/y$ is."))

R.append((r"""\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:regime}.} Regime~H follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:valueshift}. In Regime~C, holders (cost $r_h$) and the owner of the reproduced asset (cost $k_t$) Bertrand-compete to supply a service that users value at $w(p_c)$. The lower-cost side wins at a price equal to the higher of the two costs, capped at $w(p_c)$; this gives $P_C$. If $r_h<k_t$, holders serve the market at $\min\{w,k_t\}$ and their premium is $\min\{w,k_t\}-r_h\le k_t$. Otherwise they are undercut and earn no premium, and the owner's margin is $\min\{w,r_h\}-k_t$. An earlier draft stated that the owner earns $w(p_c)-k_t$. That holds only if the owner can price-discriminate or integrates downstream, which the model does not assume; the error was found in independent review (round 3). $\square$""",
r"""\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:regime}.} Regime~H follows from Proposition~\ref{prop:valueshift}. In Regime~C, holders (cost $r_h$) and the owner of the reproduced asset (cost $k_t$) compete in prices to supply a service that users value at $w(p_c)$ per unit. (i) No seller supplies below its cost and no user pays above $w(p_c)$, so if both costs exceed $w(p_c)$ there is no trade. (ii) Otherwise the lower-cost side can profitably serve the market. In the Bertrand outcome it charges the higher of the two costs, capped at $w(p_c)$: at a higher price the rival would undercut, and the cap is users' willingness to pay. This gives $P_C$. If $r_h\le k_t$, holders supply at $\min\{w,k_t\}$ and their premium is $\min\{w,k_t\}-r_h\le k_t$. If $k_t<r_h$, holders are undercut and earn no premium, and the owner's margin is $\min\{w,r_h\}-k_t$. (iii) Once $w(p_c)\ge k_t$ the premium in either case does not depend on $w(p_c)$, hence not on $p_c$, and it is at most $k_t$, which tends to zero. $\square$"""))

R.append((r"(c) The household buys if and only if $p\le R-\theta\pi L$. Its realised gain is $R-p-\pi L$, which is negative if and only if $p>R-\pi L$. The interval is non-empty because $(1-\theta)\pi L>0$. Its length $(1-\theta(v))e(1-v)L$ is a product of non-negative, non-increasing functions of $v$.",
          r"(c) The household buys if and only if $p\le R-\theta\pi L$. Its realised gain is $R-p-\pi L$, which is negative if and only if $p>R-\pi L$. The interval is non-empty because $(1-\theta)\pi L>0$, which needs both $\theta<1$ and $\pi>0$. Its intersection with $p\ge0$ is non-empty if and only if its upper end $R-\theta\pi L$ is non-negative. Its length $(1-\theta(v))e(1-v)L$ is a product of non-negative, non-increasing functions of $v$."))
R.append((r"(d) Under (d1), $\tilde p=p_I$ is constant up to the cutoff. Lemma~\ref{lem:schedule} on that segment reduces to (d2). The incumbent condition holds because $p_I<R(y_L)\le P_0$. At the cutoff $\tilde p$ is continuous. Above the cutoff, (d3) is the lemma itself.",
          r"(d) Under (d1), the effective price is $\tilde p=p_I$, constant, up to the cutoff income at which $q=eL(v_I-v)$. Every household on that segment buys, because (d2) gives $p_I\le(1-\eta)R(y_L)<R(y)$. Lemma~\ref{lem:schedule} on that segment reduces to $p_I\le(1-\eta)R(y)$ for entrants, which (d2) gives at $y_L$ and hence everywhere. Incumbents on the segment satisfy the incumbent condition because $p_I<R(y_L)\le P_0$. At the cutoff $\tilde p$ is continuous, since $p_I=p_e$ there. Above the cutoff, (d3) is the lemma itself."))
R.append((r"(e) The perceived benefit of verification, $eL[\theta(1-v)-(1-v_I)]$, falls short of the true benefit, $eL(v_I-v)$, by $eL(1-\theta)(1-v)>0$. $\square$",
          r"(e) $b(y)-\hat b(y)=eL(1-\theta)(1-v)>0$ when $\theta<1$ and $v<1$. A household for which intermediation is efficient ($q\le b$) does not buy it if and only if $q>\hat b$, which gives the ``if and only if''. (e1) is a restatement. For (e2), with $q=0$ we need $0\le b$, true for $v<v_I$, and $\hat b<0$, which is $\theta(v)(1-v)<1-v_I$. At $v=v_I$ the left side is $\theta(v_I)(1-v_I)<1-v_I$ when $\theta(v_I)<1$, so by continuity the inequality holds on a left neighbourhood of $v_I$. Under default routing every household with $q\le b(y)$ is served, which is (d1). $\square$"))

R.append((r"\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:market}.} Households gain $-Q\,dp^*/dk=\rho Q$ per unit of cost decline. By the envelope theorem $d\Pi^*/dk=-Q$. The domestic share of profit is $1-f$. The density result substitutes $F+A$ for $F$ in (d2). $\square$",
          r"\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:market}.} To first order, households gain $-Q\,dp^*/dk=\rho Q$ per unit of cost decline. By the envelope theorem $d\Pi^*/dk=-Q$. The domestic share of profit is $1-f$. Both are derivatives, so the stated changes hold up to $o(|dk|)$. Because $A$ is a fixed amount per intermediary, it adds to the fixed cost: the zero-profit fee is $m+(F+A)/n$, and substituting it in (d2) gives (d2$_A$). $\square$"))

R.append((r"""\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:apprentice}.} A firm hires a novice if and only if $y_N-w_N-\kappa_j\ge y_N-p_c$. With $w_N\ge0$ binding at $w_N=0$, this is $\kappa_j\le p_c$, so $a^m=G(p_c)$. Socially, an apprenticeship is worth $p_c+\beta W$ against cost $\kappa_j$, so $a^*=G(p_c+\beta W)$. If novices could pay up to $\beta W$, the market would attain $a^*$. Each cohort's experts are the previous cohort's apprentices. With a subsidy $s$, firms hire if $\kappa_j\le p_c+s$. With $G$ uniform, $a^*-a^m=\beta W/\bar\kappa$ and the loss is $\int_{p_c}^{p_c+\beta W}(p_c+\beta W-\kappa)/\bar\kappa\,d\kappa=(\beta W)^2/(2\bar\kappa)$, both decreasing in $p_c$ because $W$ is. $\square$""",
r"""\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:apprentice}.} A firm hires a novice if and only if $y_N-w_N-\kappa_j\ge y_N-p_c$. With $w_N\ge0$ binding at $w_N=0$, this is $\kappa_j\le p_c$, so firms demand $G(p_c)$. Socially, an apprenticeship is worth $p_c+\beta W$ against cost $\kappa_j$, so the efficient demand is $G(p_c+\beta W)$. Because capacity does not bind, $\chi T_{t-1}\ge a^*\ge a^m$, equation~\eqref{eq:capacity} gives $T_t=a_t$ and the rates are attained. Strictness: $W>0$ and $p_c<\bar\kappa$ give $p_c<\min\{p_c+\beta W,\bar\kappa\}$, and $g>0$ on that interval gives $G(p_c+\beta W)>G(p_c)$ and a positive loss. The loss is the integral of the social net value $p_c+\beta W-\kappa$ over the apprenticeships the market omits, which are those with $\kappa\in(p_c,\min\{p_c+\beta W,\bar\kappa\}]$. With a subsidy $s$, firms hire if $\kappa_j\le p_c+s$: $s^*=\beta W$ gives $a^*$, and $s=\bar p-p_c$ gives $G(\bar p)$, which is attained when $\chi T_{t-1}\ge G(\bar p)$. With $G$ uniform and $p_c+\beta W\le\bar\kappa$, no truncation occurs, so $a^*-a^m=\beta W/\bar\kappa$ and the loss is $\int_{p_c}^{p_c+\beta W}(p_c+\beta W-\kappa)/\bar\kappa\,d\kappa=(\beta W)^2/(2\bar\kappa)$. Both are increasing in $W$, which is decreasing in $p_c$. $\square$

\paragraph{Proof of Corollary~\ref{cor:capacity}.} (i) If $T=\min\{a,\chi T\}>0$ is stationary and $\chi<1$, then $\chi T<T$ and $T=\min\{a,\chi T\}\le\chi T<T$, a contradiction. With $\chi\ge1$, $\chi T\ge T$, so $T=a$. (ii) $T_1=\min\{a_L,\chi a_H\}=a_L$ because $\chi\ge1$. Suppose $T_{t}=\min\{a_H,\chi^{t-1}a_L\}$ for some $t\ge1$. Then $T_{t+1}=\min\{a_H,\chi T_t\}=\min\{a_H,\chi a_H,\chi^{t}a_L\}=\min\{a_H,\chi^{t}a_L\}$, using $\chi a_H\ge a_H$. With $\chi=1$ this is $a_L$ for every $t$. With $\chi>1$, $T_t=a_H$ once $\chi^{t-1}\ge a_H/a_L$. (iii) The cumulative shortfall is $(a_H-a_L)+\sum_{t\ge2}(a_H-T_t)$. Each later term is non-negative and the $t=2$ term is positive if and only if $\chi a_L<a_H$; if $\chi a_L\ge a_H$, all later terms are zero. (iv) $T_t\le\chi T_{t-1}$ for every $t$, so $T_t\le\chi^tT_0$. $\square$"""))

R.append((r"\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:liability}.} The CARA certainty equivalent of a loss lottery, $RP=\lambda^{-1}\ln(1-\pi+\pi e^{\lambda L})-\pi L$, is increasing in $\lambda$ \citep{pratt1964risk}. Composing with $\lambda'<0$ gives a premium decreasing in $y$. The comparison with $\pi L(1+\ell)$ is immediate. $\square$",
          r"\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:liability}.} (i) $RP=\lambda^{-1}\ln(1-\pi+\pi e^{\lambda L})-\pi L$ is the CARA risk premium of the loss lottery, the amount by which the certainty-equivalent cost of bearing the loss exceeds its expected value $\pi L$. For a non-degenerate lottery ($0<\pi<1$, $L>0$) it is positive by Jensen's inequality and strictly increasing in $\lambda$ \citep{pratt1964risk}. Composing with $\lambda'<0$ gives a premium strictly decreasing in $y$. (ii) Self-bearing costs $\pi L+RP(y)$ in certainty-equivalent terms, and pooling costs $(1+\ell)\pi L$. With $\pi$ common, $RP(y)$ is decreasing and $\ell\pi L$ is constant, so the set where $RP(y)\ge\ell\pi L$ is a lower interval. (iii) Under pooling the household bears no risk, so the risk premium disappears from its effective price. The remaining term, $(1+\ell)\pi(y)L$, has derivative $-(1+\ell)eLv'(y)$, which is the gradient in Proposition~\ref{prop:verification} scaled by $1+\ell$. $\square$"))

R.append((r"\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis}.} The current relative gain is the sum of the consumer component, which is non-increasing by Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d) and Lemma~\ref{lem:schedule} under C2 and C4, and the factor-income component $\Delta w\,\tau(y)/y$, which is non-increasing because C3 makes $\Delta w$ common and non-negative and C1 makes $\tau(y)/y$ non-increasing. (A common $\Delta w<0$ would reverse the monotonicity; this is why C3 is needed separately, as the round-3 review pointed out.) A sum of non-increasing functions is non-increasing. Under C5, $a\ge G(\bar p)$. The weak-necessity examples are constructed and verified in \texttt{code/necessity\_examples.py}. $\square$",
r"""\paragraph{Proof of Proposition~\ref{prop:synthesis}.} \emph{Consumer part.} C2 gives (d1) and (d3) and C4 gives (d2$_A$), with the maintained assumptions $R(y_L)\le P_0$ and a positive denominator. Under pooled liability the risk-premium term is absent (Proposition~\ref{prop:liability}(iii)), so the effective-price schedule is that of Proposition~\ref{prop:verification}(d) with fee $q_A$. That proposition gives $EV_{\text{cons}}(y)/y$ non-increasing.

\emph{Factor-income part.} By C3, AI is non-autonomous in both states. Since $s_j$ is increasing in $z_A$ and $C$ is decreasing in $D$, $C(D^0)<s^0_{\min}$ implies $C(D^1)<s^1_{\min}$, so every holder is employed in both states and $w_T^k(z)=z_A^k+(1-z_A^k)z-C(D^k)$. Hence
\[
\Delta w_T(y)=(z_A^1-z_A^0)\,(1-z(y))+[C(D^0)-C(D^1)].
\]
Both terms are non-negative. The first is non-increasing in $y$ because $z(y)$ is non-decreasing (C3), and the second does not depend on $y$. With $\Delta w_P=w(p_c^1)-w(p_c^0)\ge0$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:valueshift}(i)), $g(y)=\Delta w_P+\Delta w_T(y)$ is non-negative and non-increasing. By C1, $h(y)=\tau(y)/y$ is non-negative and non-increasing. For $y<y'$, $g(y)h(y)\ge g(y')h(y)\ge g(y')h(y')$, using $h(y)\ge0$ for the first inequality and $g(y')\ge0$ for the second. So $\tau(y)g(y)/y$ is non-increasing.

\emph{Sum and next cohort.} A sum of non-increasing functions is non-increasing, so $\Gamma(y)/y$ is non-increasing. By C5, $G(p_c+s)\ge G(\bar p)=T_0$ and $\chi T_0\ge T_0$, so $T_1=\min\{G(p_c+s),\chi T_0\}\ge T_0$.

\emph{Weak necessity.} The eight parameterisations of Table~\ref{tab:necessity} are defined in \texttt{code/prop8.py}. For each, \texttt{code/verify\_propositions.py} evaluates every condition and the maintained assumptions on the income grid, confirms that only the targeted condition fails, and confirms that $\Gamma(y)/y$ increases on part of the grid or that $T_1<T_0$. This establishes the existence claim for those parameterisations. $\square$"""))

